Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Check every possible starting index in haystack.
- Compare characters one by one with needle.
- If all characters match, return the starting index.

Algorithm:
1. Traverse i from 0 to n-m.
2. Compare haystack[i+j] with needle[j].
3. If all characters match, return i.
4. If no match is found, return -1.

Time Complexity: O((n - m + 1) × m) ≈ O(nm)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int strStr(string haystack, string needle) {
        int n = haystack.size();
        int m = needle.size();

        for (int i = 0; i <= n - m; i++) {
            int j = 0;

            while (j < m && haystack[i + j] == needle[j])
                j++;

            if (j == m)
                return i;
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
Idea:
- Use the built-in `find()` function provided by C++ STL.
- It internally uses an optimized string searching algorithm.

Algorithm:
1. Call haystack.find(needle).
2. If found, return the index.
3. Otherwise, return -1.

Time Complexity: Average O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int strStr(string haystack, string needle) {
        int pos = haystack.find(needle);

        return (pos == string::npos) ? -1 : pos;
    }
};
```

Optimal Solution
----------------
Idea:
- Use the KMP (Knuth-Morris-Pratt) Algorithm.
- Preprocess the pattern (needle) to build the LPS (Longest Prefix Suffix) array.
- While matching, use the LPS array to avoid rechecking already matched characters.

Algorithm:
1. Build the LPS array for the needle.
2. Traverse both strings using two pointers.
3. If characters match, move both pointers.
4. If mismatch occurs:
   - Use LPS to shift the pattern.
   - Do not move the haystack pointer unnecessarily.
5. Return the first matching index or -1.

Time Complexity: O(n + m)
Space Complexity: O(m)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> buildLPS(string pattern) {
        int m = pattern.size();
        vector<int> lps(m, 0);

        int len = 0;
        int i = 1;

        while (i < m) {
            if (pattern[i] == pattern[len]) {
                len++;
                lps[i] = len;
                i++;
            } else {
                if (len != 0)
                    len = lps[len - 1];
                else
                    i++;
            }
        }

        return lps;
    }

    int strStr(string haystack, string needle) {
        int n = haystack.size();
        int m = needle.size();

        if (m == 0)
            return 0;

        vector<int> lps = buildLPS(needle);

        int i = 0, j = 0;

        while (i < n) {
            if (haystack[i] == needle[j]) {
                i++;
                j++;

                if (j == m)
                    return i - j;
            } else {
                if (j != 0)
                    j = lps[j - 1];
                else
                    i++;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

Interview Notes
---------------
- Brute force checks every possible starting position and may compare the same characters repeatedly.
- `find()` is convenient but interviewers usually expect you to implement the algorithm yourself.
- KMP preprocesses the pattern using the LPS (Longest Prefix Suffix) array.
- LPS tells how many characters can be reused after a mismatch, avoiding unnecessary comparisons.
- Edge Cases:
  - needle is empty.
  - needle longer than haystack.
  - Exact match.
  - Multiple occurrences (return the first one).
  - No occurrence.
- Follow-up:
  - What is the LPS array?
    - `lps[i]` stores the length of the longest proper prefix that is also a suffix for the substring `needle[0...i]`.
  - Other string matching algorithms:
    - Rabin-Karp (Rolling Hash)
    - Z Algorithm
    - Boyer-Moore
- KMP is one of the most important string algorithms for coding interviews and competitive programming.
